WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:05.980
Did you know there was a remake of Psycho in 1998 starring Vince Vaughn as Norman Bates?

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.920
- Think... I think we're all in our private traps.
Clamped in 'em.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.900
It was a commercial and critical failure.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.160
It's more or less a shot-for-shot remake,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.300
which makes for a cool case study
into how the original Psycho worked so well.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.940
We can compare the two movies and look at the subtle differences, and see why those differences matter,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.420
and why it made the orignial Psycho so much better.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.440
Let's talk specifically about the ending.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.320
How did the original end?
What did Hitchcock leave us with?

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.860
Most people remember the look to the camera
from Norman Bates,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.580
but it really ends with
the car getting pulled out of the mud.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.380
The shot leaves a very bad taste in our mouths
because we know what's in the trunk.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.070
Not to mention it's filmed very off-center,
and it's coming right towards us.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.140
The remake starts the ending in a similar way

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.480
but it ends by panning out to a landscape
as the credits roll.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.320
This completely ruins the ending.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.440
We're supposed to end feeling
disgusted and uncomfortable,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.780
but the pan reintroduces us back into the world,
with its familiar landscapes and cars driving by.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.720
The original Psycho ending is great
because it's sickening.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.120
If the camera moves away from the sickening image
and ends with the familiar,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.300
we're removed from the story and the murder,
and are reintroduced back into society.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.800
That's no way to end the movie.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.600
The Psycho remake ending is closer to the
"riding into the sunset" kind of movie ending.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.260
This is a kind of shot where we're leaving the original story and get reintroduced into the world.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.000
Normally it's also shot with our main characters,
who went through some sort of ordeal,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.909
physically moving away from the camera
to continue their journey.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:27.880
The Western popularized this kind of ending

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.300
but it's still very common in modern movies,
just with a few tweaks.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.100
Nowadays it can be a car instead of a horse,
or a street corner instead of a picturesque sunset.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.720
It's a very comforting ending.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.720
We see something familiar and it's a cinematic cue
that the story is over and we're back to the real world.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.520
Twelve Angry Men ends with the men,
who all came together to pronounce the kid innocent,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.560
going back into the real world;
a world where the American judicial system works,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.700
and people are only convicted
if they actually committed the crime.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.640
It's a comforting ending,
and it complements the feel-good theme

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.140
that even the most stubborn people
can listen to reason and change their minds.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.580
Albeit not so easily and not so nicely.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.080
But I'm not saying that
all riding into the sunset endings are feel-good.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.560
How about having a serial killer walk back into society?

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.240
Now that's an eerie ending.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.160
- I do wish we could chat longer, but...

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.200
- I'm having an old friend for dinner.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.600
In a very similar way, doors closing at the end
makes for a nice symbol to end the movie.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.420
There's a physical separation now, literally closing the door on an adventure. Also very common.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.060
You can even use it to separate
two main characters within the story,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.220
which is what Francis Coppola did
in perhaps the most famous movie ending of all time.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.200
Also The Searchers,
which has an opening shot that starts at the doorway,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.820
with John Wayne coming in from the desert;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.820
and then the ending shot shows him
leaving through the same doorway.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.760
This ending symbolizes our hero
returning to the wilderness from which he came.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.920
Another common ending is when
the ending shot relates to the opening shot.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.040
There's a great supercut of opening and closing shots
of movies shown side by side on Vimeo,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.840
and a lot of them relate to each other.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.240
The goal of this kind of ending is
to show a shot similar to the opening shot,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.700
but now that you've seen the movie
it should evoke a different feeling for you.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.360
The opening shot is the first impression.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.080
The movie tells its story, and the movie ends
with something similar to what it started with.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.520
The last shot will always feel different
because after the movie tells its story

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.420
the shot has an entirely different meaning.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.020
With the Godfather sequel the shot is the same
but Michael Corleone is not, and he looks it too.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.280
In Gone Girl he uses violent imagery to refer to his wife

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:41.660
- I picture cracking her lovely skull.
Unspooling her brains. Trying to get answers.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.320
And by the last shot we know why.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.780
It's a fun way to look at movies because the ending
can potentially sum up the main ideas of the movie.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.080
The difference between the opening and the closing
is that the opening shot *hints* at the main ideas,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.760
and the ending reinforces them.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.840
It's as if the director is highlighting
the most important part of the movie.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.470
So go watch your favorite movie ending again.
Maybe look for a symbol.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.020
What do you think the feather symbolizes in
Forrest Gump?

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.980
There's no right or wrong answer,
but we know what the director wants you to notice.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.720
What mood does the ending leave you with?

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.780
That's the most important feeling of the movie, because what you walk out of the theater feeling is what matters.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:15.460
So give it a try.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:20.060
And with that I leave you with a Simpsons parody
of the Godfather ending, because I love The Simpsons.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:21.560
Thanks for watching.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.480
"Vroom! Vroom vroom!"

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:49.700
*vrooming continues*

